{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.45,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R4", "R5", "R6"],
      "pins": ["REG1.24", "REG1.17"],
      "nets": ["REG_FB", "5V output (post-L1 node)"],
      "obs": "On the Power sheet, R4 (19k6) is wired between the buck's regulated output node (the L1/C7/R5-top node) and the REG_FB node, i.e. in parallel with R5 (6k8). The feedback divider therefore has an effective top leg of R5||R4 ≈ 5.05k with R6 = 1k3.",
      "expected": "The schematic's own note states Vout = 0.8*(1+R5/R6), and the spec requires a 5 V rail ('9A @ 5V max.'). With R5 = 6k8 and R6 = 1k3 that formula gives ≈5.0 V only when R4 is NOT bridging the output to FB. R4 should not sit across R5.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet: 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)'; R5 6k8 and R4 19k6 both tied from the L1 output node to the REG_FB node, R6 1k3 from REG_FB to GND"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "feedback divider relation \"Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)\" ... Block diagram caption: \"9A @ 5V max.\""}
      ],
      "impact": "The effective top feedback resistor becomes ~5.05k instead of 6.8k, so Vout ≈ 0.8*(1+5.05/1.3) ≈ 3.9 V rather than 5 V. The '5V' rail (servo/PWM power, GPS, CH330 bridges) would be under-volted and the 3.3V LDO loses input headroom, so the board behaves differently from the intended 5 V design.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The only documented deviation (the barometer RESET/EOC pin swap) is a logged bug and is not reported. However, on the Power sheet R4 (19k6) is placed across the top feedback resistor R5 of the MIC26903 divider, contradicting the schematic's own 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)' note and dropping the nominal 5 V rail to about 3.9 V, which is a deviation from the intended design."
}

